// Chillstation 13, second draft: a HUB station. The Commons sits in the
// middle with a corridor ring around it and a department on every side,
// which is the shape a station wants and the shape the first draft did
// not have -- that one put the crew in the top-left corner and grew
// south-east until it ran out of paper.
//
// Read the picture as five bands, north to south:
//
//        solar array (in space, north of the hull)
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//   |  Workshop   | Chemistry Lab  |    Cargo Bay     |  y 4..9
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//   |             T H E   C O N C O U R S E           |  y 11
//   +------+------------------------------+-----------+
//   |E| W  |                              |   E   |E|
//   |V| e  |        T H E  C O M M O N S  |   a   |V|    y 13..18
//   |A| st |          (the only tiling)   |   st  |A|
//   +------+------------------------------+-----------+
//   |             T H E   C O N C O U R S E           |  y 20
//   +----------+--------------------------+-----------+
//   | Engineer |                          |   Cargo   |
//   +----------+     A T M O S P H E R I  |   Hall    |  y 22..34
//   |   Tank Annex   |        C S         |           |
//   +-------------------------------------------------+
//
// EVERY ROOM IS PLATING BUT ONE. The Commons is the only finished deck
// on the station (glyphs '.', 'A', 'L', 'B', 'O' below); everywhere else
// the plates are off and the subfloor is the picture -- pipe, cable,
// paint and layer, read off the deck rather than out of a wiki. That is
// what Atmospherics has always looked like here and the reason it looked
// better than the rest of the station: a corridor that shows its
// plumbing is a corridor you can learn a station from. So the tiling is
// now a PLACE rather than a default, and it marks the one room the crew
// is meant to sit down in.
//
// EVERYTHING IS FED. Ten photovoltaic panels north of the hull and a
// generator and an SMES bank in Engineering carry one L2 backbone that
// reaches all nine APCs; each APC takes from it on its L2 input port
// and opens an L1 room circuit that runs under every port, every fan
// and every door feed it owns (docs/POWER.md, "Layers and ports"). The first draft
// shipped two boot warnings on purpose (an unwired room was the
// construction playground); this one ships NONE, and the playground is
// now the empty blocked-out rooms and the space around the hull.
//
// ROOM TO GROW. Three tiles of vacuum on every side of the hull, a
// corridor ring that touches all four hull faces, and two rooms (Cargo
// Bay, Cargo Hall) built, lit, wired and deliberately empty. A new
// department bolts onto the ring by knocking out one wall run and
// wiring one APC -- no re-plumbing, no re-routing, no moving the crew.
//
// THE WAY OUT is a chamber, never a hole: two cycle-linked airlocks with
// three tiles of bare plating between them, at each end of the Commons
// band. Shape copied from tgstation's MetaStation "Escape Pod One"
// (_maps/map_files/MetaStation/MetaStation.dmm, (38,147)-(38,149)): an
// /obj/machinery/door/airlock/external at each end, three
// /turf/open/floor/plating between them, hull all the way round. The
// pair is CYCLE-LINKED (`cycle_links` below, tg's
// /obj/effect/mapping_helpers/airlock/cyclelink_helper): opening either
// one shuts the other, so the chamber cannot be held open at both ends.
// That is machinery rather than crew discipline -- the station is not
// vented by one impatient crewman.
//
// WINDOWS, twenty-three of them, and every one is a grille let into a
// HOLE in the wall rather than a picture hung on it (the tile under a
// pane is '_', never '#'). Nine look north out of the rank at the solar
// array, three west out of Engineering, three east out of the Cargo
// Hall, four into the two vestibules, and four are the tank annex's
// own -- fifteen in the hull and eight inside it. They ship
// glazed, because tg maps its window galleries assembled -- an
// /obj/effect/spawner/structure/window/reinforced, not bare rods for the
// crew to fit. A glazed pane is a BARRIER: it holds a room's air exactly
// as a shut door does (tg window.dm:405 `can_atmos_pass`). Prise one out
// of the hull and that room is open to space, which is the point of
// putting one there.
//
// Atmospherics, its gas storage and the tank annex were MOVED, not
// rebuilt: the whole block came south-west out of the first draft
// unchanged, so every layer, adapter, valve and net inside it is the one
// that was commissioned there. What changed is what it hangs off -- the
// supply main now climbs into a corridor instead of a back room, and the
// scrubbers main comes home from the Commons instead of from the only
// other room on the station.
//
// THE COMMONS IS TRIMMED. It is the only finished deck on the station
// and now the only trimmed one: a blue line inside its perimeter,
// painted tile by tile, with the shape of the room worked out from which
// neighbours share it rather than authored corner by corner. The lab's
// north band and the cargo marks are the same mechanism said in the
// quadrant family and in facing marks; the two interlock chambers wear
// an uncoloured hazard box, because the one tile you should not be
// standing in when a door cycles is worth a frame of paint.
//
// Demo: docs/chillstation-v2.png.
(
    name: "Chillstation 13",
    tags: ["space"],
    // The commissioned station assumes ordinary walking. New maps may omit
    // this preference when they want the mode to choose microgravity.
    environment: (gravity_m_s2: Exact(9.80665)),
    legend: {
        ' ': (composition: (), outdoors: true),
        // The solar run crosses open space on an integrated catwalk. It
        // inherits the selected substrate and stays outdoors, while giving
        // the existing cable placements the support they require.
        '=': (composition: (components: [(component: "catwalk")]), outdoors: true),
        '#': (composition: (components: [(component: "station_base"), (component: "wall")])),
        // Bare deck, and the station's DEFAULT. Everything under it is
        // visible: pipe, cable, and the paint that tells two mains apart.
        '_': (composition: (components: [(component: "station_base")])),
        // Finished tiling, and the Commons' alone (see the header).
        '.': (composition: (components: [(component: "station_base"), (component: "floor_tiles")])),
    },
    rows: [
        "                                        ",
        "      ===================               ",
        "             =                          ",
        "   ####___########___########___#####   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #_________#___________#__________#   ",
        "   #####_##########_##########__#####   ",
        "   #________________________________#   ",
        "   #####_##########_###########_#####   ",
        "   ###________#..........#________###   ",
        "   #__________#..........___________#   ",
        "   ____________..........____________   ",
        "   #__________#..........#__________#   ",
        "   ###________#..........#________###   ",
        "   ###________#..........#________###   ",
        "   #####_##########_###########_#####   ",
        "   #________________________________#   ",
        "   #####_##########_###########_#####   ",
        "   #_________#_____________#________#   ",
        "   __________#_____________#________#   ",
        "   __________#_____________#_________   ",
        "   __________#_____________#_________   ",
        "   ###_###_###_____________#_________   ",
        "   #_________#_____________#________#   ",
        "   #_______________________#________#   ",
        "   #_______________________##########   ",
        "   #_________#######_#######            ",
        "   #_______________________#            ",
        "   #_________###_###_###_###            ",
        "   #_________##___#___#___##            ",
        "   ############___#___#___##            ",
        "             ###############            ",
        "                                        ",
        "                                        ",
        "                                        ",
    ],
    items: [
        (17, 16, "toolbox"),
        // Writing supplies share the Commons and Medical work surfaces.
        (23, 17, "paper_bin"),
        (23, 17, "toner_cartridge"),
        (23, 17, "four_color_pen"),
        (23, 17, "crayon_box"),
        (24, 17, "paper"),
        (22, 6, "paper_bin"),
        (22, 6, "toner_cartridge"),
        (22, 6, "four_color_pen"),
        (22, 6, "crayon_box"),
        (22, 6, "paper"),
        // ---- the Commons -----------------------------------------
        // A shift is long, and this is the room everybody walks
        // through. There used to be two loose dice on the floor here --
        // the only things on this map that did nothing useful, which
        // was the whole of what they were for. They are inside the Good
        // Clean Fun now, in the bag tg keeps them in, along with a deck
        // of cards and a toy that points at a wall. A die on the deck
        // is one die; a machine that hands out dice is a rec room.
        // ---- the Chemistry Lab -----------------------------------
        // Two beakers and a burner on the deck under the dispenser and
        // the reaction chamber. The beakers because chemistry is two
        // vessels and a pour, and the burner because the chamber is
        // bolted to a wall and a flame is not -- it is how you heat the
        // thing you are carrying, and it arrives with fifteen units of
        // spirit in it (items/burner.luau).
        (19, 5, "beaker"),
        (19, 5, "beaker"),
        // And a third with a lump of ice in it, out of the freezer and
        // sitting in a 20 C lab: matter changing state with nobody
        // touching it, on the bench where somebody would notice.
        (19, 5, "beaker", { "blend": "ice_block" }),
        (20, 5, "burner"),
        // A two-litre pressure flask and its adjacent syngas canister. The
        // flask is reachable from the medical-side spawn and can be held
        // against the canister for direct gas charging.
        (17, 6, "lab_flask"),
        (18, 5, "resin_pellet", { "blend": "resin_pellet_charge" }),
        (18, 5, "resin_pellet", { "blend": "resin_pellet_charge" }),
        // The small glass, on the tile beside them. A dropper for
        // moving one unit at a time, two tubes to keep a batch in while
        // the beaker is needed for the next one, and a bottle to put
        // the finished thing away in. Every one of them shows its level
        // through its own glass, which is the whole reason a bench of
        // vessels reads at a glance instead of being clicked through.
        (21, 5, "dropper"),
        (21, 5, "bottle"),
        (22, 5, "test_tube"),
        (22, 5, "test_tube"),
        // ---- the Workshop ----------------------------------------
        // Everything the first draft scattered across an unfinished
        // room, now in the room whose job it is: sheet, tile, rod,
        // glass, the tools to fit them and the boards that turn a frame
        // into a machine. The rank's west room is where a shift learns
        // to build, and it is one door off the Concourse.
        (5, 6, "welder"),
        (6, 6, "crowbar"),
        (5, 7, "cable_coil"),
        (6, 7, "cable_coil"),
        (7, 6, "metal"),
        (7, 7, "metal"),
        (8, 6, "floor_tile"),
        (8, 7, "floor_tile"),
        (9, 6, "machine_board.vendor"),
        (10, 6, "machine_board.tool_vendor"),
        (11, 6, "machine_board.games_vendor"),
        (9, 7, "machine_board.tv"),
        (11, 7, "machine_board.nanomed_plus"),
        (12, 7, "machine_board.chemdrobe"),
        (5, 8, "rod"),
        (6, 8, "glass"),
        (7, 8, "rglass"),
        (8, 8, "screwdriver"),
        // Two sheets of reinforced glass, stacked, as the gallery's
        // spare. The windows themselves ship glazed; what a shift
        // actually needs on hand is the glass to put one BACK.
        (9, 8, "rglass"),
        (9, 8, "rglass"),
        // Lighting stock: a shattered tube is something that happens, so
        // something to fix it with is something the station needs.
        (10, 7, "box_lights"),
        (10, 8, "light_replacer"),
        // And the janitor's other tool, in the same corner: a station
        // that can spill a beaker needs something to answer one with.
        (10, 8, "mop"),
        // A charged CO2 extinguisher belongs by the Workshop's tools;
        // it can be carried to the adjacent EVA vestibule and refilled
        // from the engineering canister valve.
        (11, 8, "fire_extinguisher"),
        // ---- the atmospherics bench ------------------------------
        // The four things a technician actually carries. The RPD builds
        // and removes plumbing anywhere, the analyser reads a room or a
        // run, the painter is how a main gets its livery -- and paint
        // here is plumbing, so it is also how a main gets cut by mistake
        // -- and the spare meter is a gauge for wherever the next
        // question is.
        (15, 29, "rpd"),
        (16, 29, "analyzer"),
        (17, 29, "pipe_painter"),
        (18, 29, "pipe_meter"),
        (19, 29, "wrench"),
        // And the other painter on the same bench: the deck's livery
        // rather than the main's. The station ships already trimmed, so
        // these two are for changing somebody's mind about it -- the
        // eraser beside the painter, because the tool that makes a
        // marking and the only tool that unmakes one belong together.
        (20, 29, "decal_painter"),
        (21, 29, "decal_eraser"),
        // ---- the west EVA vestibule ------------------------------
        // The suit and the bottle, standing in the chamber they are
        // for. Everybody wakes up with a breath mask and three litres
        // of oxygen; this is the OTHER half of going outside -- seventy
        // litres to breathe and a garment that keeps the vacuum off the
        // skin, because a tank alone stops you suffocating and does
        // nothing about the pressure.
        //
        // The pairing is tg's: the engineer's EVA outfit issues the
        // space suit and the standard oxygen tank together
        // (code/modules/jobs/job_types/station_engineer.dm:75-82).
        // West rather than east because west is the side Engineering
        // and the Workshop are on, and the crew who go outside come
        // from those two rooms.
        (4, 15, "eva_suit"),
        (4, 15, "oxygen_tank"),
        // ---- the rack room --------------------------------------
        // Two blank disks on the deck between the router and the
        // terminal, which is where somebody who had been using them
        // would have put them down. The vendor sells more
        // (machines/tool_vendor.luau); these are the two that mean a
        // shift can start writing without walking to the Commons first.
        (10, 27, "floppy_disk"),
        (11, 27, "floppy_disk"),
    ],
    structures: [
        // Medical-side Chemistry Lab floor, beside the operating computer
        // and table at (23, 5) and (24, 5).
        (23, 6, "player_spawn", { "weight": 99, "jobs": "medic" }),
        // The lab's portable syngas supply: loose on open floor, so a
        // chemist can hold the flask at its valve without a pipe connector.
        (16, 6, "canister", { "blend": "syngas_6000kpa" }),
        // Commons floor is the station's unrestricted social spawn area.
        (18, 16, "player_spawn", { "weight": 1 }),
        (19, 16, "player_spawn"),
        (20, 16, "player_spawn"),
        (21, 16, "player_spawn"),
        // The Workshop's tool locker. Which tools happen to be inside
        // is rolled from structures/locker.luau at roundstart; the map
        // places one variant and carries no duplicate loot vocabulary.
        (11, 5, "locker.tools"),
        // ---- the cadaver chamber rank --------------------------------------
        // Three chambers on the Chemistry Lab's west wall, opposite the
        // medical vendors already standing on its north one. This
        // station has no ward, so the room that holds the bench and the
        // medicine is where the dead wait. All three face EAST into the
        // aisle: a drawer needs exactly one open tile in front of it
        // for its slab, and east is the direction tg's own morgues
        // point (morgue.dm:234, the only varedit its mappers make).
        // Standing them in a rank with one lane in front of the whole
        // row is tg's mapping too.
        //
        // The middle one is the `cadaver` variant: it opens the shift
        // already occupied. tg fills three trays per morgue area from a
        // roundstart helper (mapping_helpers.dm:920-969); one drawer in
        // three on a station this size is the same idea at this scale.
        (14, 6, "cadaver_chamber", { "dir": "east" }),
        (14, 7, "cadaver_chamber.cadaver", { "dir": "east" }),
        (14, 8, "cadaver_chamber", { "dir": "east" }),
        // The operating table, east of the operating computer at
        // (23, 5) and so inside the four tiles that console reads. It
        // is the only surface in the station worth cutting somebody
        // open on -- everywhere else is deck or an ordinary table --
        // and it stands in the open on purpose: a surgeon works from
        // the tile beside it, and a body is dragged onto it from any
        // of the three.
        (24, 5, "optable"),
        // ---- the hull galleries ----------------------------------
        // Nine panes across the north rank, looking out at the solar
        // array; three west out of Engineering; three east out of the
        // Cargo Hall. Each is a hole in the hull with glass in it, so
        // each is a place the station can be opened by a crowbar rather
        // than by a bomb -- which is exactly why a station has them.
        (7, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (8, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (9, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (18, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (19, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (20, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (29, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (30, 3, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (31, 3, "grille", "reinforced"), (3, 23, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (3, 24, "grille", "reinforced"), (3, 25, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (36, 24, "grille", "reinforced"), (36, 25, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (36, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // And four into the two vestibules, so the halls can see who is
        // in the chamber and whether the outer door is open before it
        // matters. Same glass, same consequence if it goes: these are
        // all that stand between a corridor and a chamber that is
        // deliberately opened to space several times a shift.
        (5, 14, "grille", "reinforced"), (5, 16, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (34, 14, "grille", "reinforced"), (34, 16, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // ---- the air the crew breathes ---------------------------
        // Eight vents, one per room the crew stands in, each on the end
        // of a spur off the distribution main that rings the station.
        // They are ordinary machines placed like every other machine in
        // the plumbing -- a technician can unbolt one, carry it away and
        // wrench it down somewhere else, and the RPD will hand out more.
        //
        // The Cargo Bay is the one room with no fan, and that is the
        // expansion stub: distro reaches its wall and stops, so the
        // next department starts with one length of pipe and one vent
        // rather than with a survey.
        (11, 8, "vent"), (17, 8, "vent"), (7, 17, "vent"),
        (18, 17, "vent"), (22, 17, "vent"), (31, 14, "vent"),
        (6, 23, "vent"), (34, 23, "vent"),
        // ---- the Commons' card table ------------------------------
        // Two tables in the room's south-east corner, directly under
        // the Good Clean Fun and clear of both rows a shift walks the
        // Commons along. They are two rather than one because what a
        // station built out of separate squares does when you put two
        // down -- read as one surface -- ought to be visible in the
        // room a shift actually crosses (tg's `SMOOTH_GROUP_TABLES`,
        // tables_racks.dm:29-31). And because the machine that hands
        // out a deck of cards now has somewhere to deal onto: a card
        // put down on a table lands on the pixel it was pointed at,
        // which is what makes a hand of cards a hand rather than a
        // pile.
        (23, 17, "table"), (24, 17, "table"),
        (22, 6, "table"),
        // The scrubber that makes the department a LOOP rather than a
        // supply: it stands on the Commons' own room circuit, on L1,
        // and takes the carbon dioxide the crew breathe out back to
        // Atmospherics.
        //
        // It says its posture out loud because it is NOT the posture a
        // placed scrubber gets. A grille dropped on a deck today is a
        // return grille -- whole air, gently, down to a floor just
        // under the vent's release line -- which is what a hull that
        // radiates wants once there is a heating loop to feed. This one
        // is the department's CO2 recovery leg and predates that: it is
        // pointed at the one gas, dial wide open, taking it to the last
        // mole, and the chamber at the far end of the filter row is
        // where that gas ends up.
        (16, 17, "air_scrubber", "scrubbing", { "whole_air": false, "rate_lps": 200.0, "floor_kpa": 0.0 }),
        // THE BOTTLE RACK. Four sockets in the west hall, each beside
        // the through-main and mouthing south into it: the connector is
        // the component on its own tile, never raw pipe hidden under
        // one. Pull a port up with a wrench and that bottle stops
        // charging the ring.
        //
        // Four rather than the first draft's one, and the number is not
        // decoration -- it is the ring measured. That draft ran twenty
        // tiles of distribution between two rooms, and one bottle could
        // fill twenty tiles several times over. This one rings the
        // whole station: seventy-five tiles of layer-3 distro under the
        // Concourse and its spurs, which is very nearly four times as
        // much pipe to hold up. A reserve is sized against its loop or
        // it is sized against nothing, and the first hull breach is
        // where you find out which. This is the room where that
        // arithmetic is visible, standing on the deck in a row.
        (8, 17, "connector_port"),
        (9, 17, "connector_port"),
        (10, 17, "connector_port"),
        (11, 17, "connector_port"),
        // ---- where the department meets the station --------------
        // The supply main leaves Atmospherics through its own north
        // wall, crosses the south corridor in the open, and becomes the
        // station's distribution run. Three fittings, in a row, on deck
        // a technician can stand on:
        //   (20, 20) the isolation valve on the main, which is where
        //            tg puts one and what the wiki tells a technician
        //            to reach for first -- a valve wherever you might
        //            need to part a line without stopping the station.
        //            A per-layer preset, because a valve takes its
        //            layer from its entry and a main is not on the
        //            default one. It ships OPEN: a commissioned
        //            station's valves are, and an isolation point is a
        //            thing you CAN shut, not a thing that starts shut.
        //   (22, 20) the layer adapter that puts the layer-4 main onto
        //            the layer-3 run the station's vents are plumbed
        //            to. An adapter welds every layer it touches into
        //            ONE net; take it out with a wrench and the ring
        //            parts from the department, conserving every mole
        //            (tfs/tests/adapters_test.luau).
        //   (13, 20) the scrubbers main's own wheel, on the other side
        //            of the corridor and on layer 2, so the waste side
        //            can be cut without touching supply.
        (20, 20, "manual_valve", "open", { "channel": "l4" }),
        (22, 20, "layer_adapter"),
        (13, 20, "manual_valve", "open", { "channel": "l2" }),

        // ---- Atmospherics ----------------------------------------
        // Everything from here down came out of the first draft
        // unchanged, moved as one block. GAS STORAGE: two bottles on
        // connector ports feed the process line; two more hang off the
        // machines that need or make a second gas -- the mixer's other
        // intake, and the bottle the filter fills with what it pulls
        // out.
        // The chamber feed's bottles are STORAGE, not regulators: a
        // fastened bottle is one body with its line (tg's connector
        // coupling), and the chamber injectors have no ceiling -- so
        // the pair's header is bounded by two pumps holding the feed
        // at one atmosphere, which is the regulator job tg gives a
        // pump and never a bottle.
        (14, 25, "pump", "running", { "dir": "south" }),
        (18, 23, "pump", "running", { "dir": "east", "target_kpa": 4500.0 }),
        (15, 22, "connector_port"),
        (17, 22, "connector_port"),
        (23, 26, "connector_port"),
        // THE PROCESS LINE, west to east. Everything the department does
        // to gas happens along one row of deck: separate, blend, push.
        // The filter's side mouth drops south into the bottle it fills;
        // the mixer's takes from the one beside it. Commissioned, not
        // shipped in a box: the map places each of these in a STATE, and
        // for a machine that has positions the state IS the switch. tg
        // maps `/on` subtypes for exactly this reason -- a station whose
        // air only starts moving once somebody walks to Atmospherics is
        // not a station.
        // A filter ROW, tg's shape: one machine per gas, in series down
        // the waste line, each dropping what it catches out of its side
        // mouth. Oxygen first, then carbon dioxide; what survives both
        // is the nitrogen the mixer blends back.
        (19, 23, "filter.n2", "running"),
        (21, 23, "filter.co2", "running"),
        (23, 23, "mixer.airmix", "running"),
        (25, 23, "pump", "running"),
        // Two gauges, either side of the machines that change the gas --
        // which is the only place a gauge earns its bolt.
        (20, 23, "meter"),
        (24, 23, "meter"),
        // THE LAYER ADAPTERS, and the reason the department can exist at
        // all. The station's vents are on piping layer 3
        // (structures/vent.luau, the distribution layer), while tg's
        // mains are layer 4 for supply and layer 2 for scrubbers. So:
        //   (26, 24) puts the pump's layer-3 outlet onto the layer-4
        //            supply main;
        //   (17, 27) brings the layer-2 scrubbers main home into the
        //            layer-3 process line, so scrubbed air is
        //            re-filtered rather than dumped;
        //   (25, 27) is the storage loop's own -- it puts the layer-2
        //            chamber draw onto the layer-3 line into the mixer's
        //            side intake.
        // (The fourth, at (22, 20) above, is the one that leaves the
        // department; it is listed with the station's fittings because
        // that is whose side of the bulkhead it is on.)
        (26, 24, "layer_adapter"),
        (17, 27, "layer_adapter"),
        (25, 27, "layer_adapter"),
        // The COLOUR adapter, joining the cyan process line to the green
        // chamber feed. Paint is plumbing here -- two runs whose colours
        // disagree are two networks -- so this is the one fitting that
        // lets a department keep its lines legible and still have them
        // be one body of gas.
        (14, 24, "color_adapter"),
        // ISOLATION, inside the department: tg puts a hand valve wherever
        // a technician needs to cut a leg out without stopping the
        // station, and this is the one on the green feed that charges the
        // oxygen and nitrogen chambers. The manual valve is the wheel you
        // turn in the dark; the digital one answers its panel and needs
        // the lights on. Both ship OPEN.
        (14, 28, "manual_valve", "open"),
        (16, 23, "digital_valve", "open"),
        // The waste-side pump, on the return riser: the scrubbers main
        // arrives here having crossed the station on its own and needs
        // pushing up into the process line.
        //
        // A VOLUMETRIC one, and that is the whole point of the choice. A
        // pressure pump works to a setpoint and stops -- put one here at
        // one atmosphere, against a process line already at one
        // atmosphere, and it has arrived before it started and the
        // return never moves. A return line does not want a pressure, it
        // wants a throughput, which is exactly what tg's volume pump is
        // for (volume_pump.dm: it "never asks").
        // A PRESSURE pump, deliberately: the waste lift runs the main down
        // to nothing, and a volumetric pump refuses an input that thin
        // (tg volume_pump.dm's input_starting_pressure < 0.01 cutoff) --
        // so the last trickle of a scrubber's catch parked in the main
        // forever. A pump moves moles toward its setpoint no matter
        // how thin the source, which is what a waste lift is for.
        (18, 26, "pump", "running", { "dir": "north", "target_kpa": 4500.0 }),
        // Bottled nitrogen off the first filter's side mouth. The
        // connector stands at (19,25) and mouths north into the catch
        // pipe at (19,24); that keeps the component off the raw run and
        // keeps its own tile's valid layer-4 crossing intact.
        (19, 25, "connector_port"),
        // ---- the tank annex --------------------------------------
        // Seven stationary reservoirs, one per substance this shift's
        // atmospherics runs on, each filled by the PLACEMENT rather than
        // by a prototype of its own -- tg ships twenty /tank/<gas>
        // subtypes and this map ships none. 23 000 kPa is tg's
        // `starting_pressure_percent = 0.5` of the 46 000 kPa rating
        // (tank.dm:39, 43).
        //
        // Deliberately UNPLUMBED. A tank shares its contents with every
        // net its four mouths reach, so a shared main through this row
        // would settle all seven into one mixture within a pass and the
        // store would be seven tanks of the same thing. The gaps between
        // them and the whole of rows 29-30 and 32 are where an engineer
        // runs pipe to whichever one they want today.
        (4, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "o2_23000kpa" }),
        (6, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "n2_23000kpa" }),
        (8, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "co2_23000kpa" }),
        (10, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "plasma_23000kpa" }),
        // The fifth is the WATER reservoir, and it is the odd one in the
        // row because water is the one substance here with a liquid
        // phase: there is no `water_vapor` to bottle, only water, which
        // at annex temperature belongs in the bottom of the tank rather
        // than hanging in it.
        //
        // A fill names a PRESSURE at T20C and the moles follow from
        // PV = nRT, so the arithmetic runs backwards from how full the
        // shell should be:
        //   half of 2 500 L of liquid = 1 250 L = 1 250 kg,
        //   1 250 000 g / 18.015 g/mol = 69 386 mol -- call it 69 000;
        //   69 000 x 8.314 x 293.15 / 2.5 m^3 = 67.27e6 Pa.
        // Hence 67 268 kPa, which is 69 000 mol, which is 1 243 L of
        // water once it has condensed: a tank a bit under half full,
        // with damp air over it.
        //
        // Plumb this one into a warm room and it RAINS -- the water
        // leaves as vapour, crosses the dew point on the far side, and
        // lands on the deck. That is the intended experiment, and it is
        // why this tank stands at the east end of the row, on the side
        // the corridor door is on.
        (12, 28, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "water_67268kpa" }),
        // The annex placard, bolted to the west wall beside the row it
        // is about. It names the plasma store's OWN blend, so what it
        // draws is that store's rating rather than the room's -- a sign
        // says what went into the chamber, once, at commissioning
        // (docs/construction/signs.md).
        (4, 29, "hazard_diamond", { "blend": "plasma_23000kpa", "mount": "west" }),
        // ...and one in the commons with nothing named on it, which is
        // a blank placard: no digits and no symbol, because a sign
        // nobody has filled in has nothing to say.
        (15, 16, "hazard_diamond", { "mount": "west" }),
        (5, 33, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "tritium_23000kpa" }),
        (7, 33, "stationary_tank", "anchored", { "blend": "n2o_23000kpa" }),
        // The temperature control unit, on the machine tile the annex
        // wired for it: the L1 room circuit under its feet, the bench run
        // stopping either side. Its one mouth auto-binds the east stub;
        // pipe from there to whichever tank is today's experiment and
        // the thermostat conditions what the tank feeds it. Ships
        // running at its T20C default like the rest of the commissioned
        // department -- a conditioner with nothing to condition idles.
        (10, 31, "thermomachine", "running"),
        // Two panes in the wall the annex shares with Engineering, so
        // the power room can read its own gas store off the deck, and
        // two in the wall it shares with the machine hall, so the tank
        // row can be read from the department. Glass stands in a HOLE in
        // the wall and never on it, which is why the four tiles under
        // these are plating.
        (6, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (10, 26, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (13, 28, "grille", "reinforced"),
        (13, 29, "grille", "reinforced"),
        // Each chamber's pair, the way tg pairs them: an injector
        // pushing gas IN off the green feed, and a scrubber set to
        // SIPHON drawing it back out onto the yellow draw. Both ship
        // running -- a storage chamber nobody switched on is a sealed
        // room full of gas with no way to reach it.
        // Aimed by hand, every one: each injector's feed drops down
        // the wall column WEST of it and its siphon partner draws off
        // the run SOUTH of it -- two joinable mouths apiece, and an
        // automatic fit that guessed the other one got a machine that
        // moved nothing. A room device has one mouth, so it is aimed by
        // where that mouth reaches ("pipe") rather than by the picture:
        // an injector whose feed is west says west.
        (15, 33, "air_injector", "injecting", { "pipe": "west" }),
        (15, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning", { "pipe": "south" }),
        (19, 33, "air_injector", "injecting", { "pipe": "west" }),
        (19, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning", { "pipe": "south" }),
        (23, 33, "air_injector", "injecting", { "pipe": "west" }),
        (23, 34, "air_scrubber", "siphoning", { "pipe": "south" }),
        // ---- EVERY THRESHOLD ON THE STATION ----------------------
        // Nineteen airlocks, placed by id like everything else that
        // stands on the deck. What used to be two glyphs ('+' inside,
        // 'E' at the vestibules) is one entry with properties: the six
        // leaves of the two EVA chambers are the four at (3, 15),
        // (5, 15), (34, 15) and (36, 15), and they read the same as
        // every other door because they ARE the same door -- a public
        // airlock with nothing said about it.
        //
        // Which livery one wears, whether somebody put a window in it
        // and who may walk through are per placement now
        // (structures/airlock.luau), so the station can paint
        // Engineering's doors without a second prototype existing.
        //
        // THREE OF THEM ARE NOT ORDINARY. Two are the same two-tile
        // leaf standing the two ways it is drawn: the Cargo Bay's
        // threshold is `airlock_wide` resting on (30, 10) and the tile
        // east of it, and the Commons' east entrance is the same entry
        // turned -- `dir: east` on (25, 15), which puts its second cell
        // one step NORTH (docs/content-schema/facings.md). Both hulls were opened by one tile
        // to take them -- a crate does not fit through a doorway a man
        // fits through, and the room the whole crew walks into should
        // not be entered single file. The EAST wall took the wide one
        // because the west wall is carrying the room's APC and its
        // placard and has no tile to spare. The third is the Commons'
        // north entrance off the concourse (`airlock.interior_glass`),
        // one leaf with a window in it, so the room the crew sits down
        // in can be seen into on the way past.
        (8, 10, "airlock"), (19, 10, "airlock"), (30, 10, "airlock_wide"),
        (19, 12, "airlock.interior_glass"), (3, 15, "airlock"),
        (5, 15, "airlock"), (14, 15, "airlock"), (25, 15, "airlock_wide", { "dir": "east" }),
        (34, 15, "airlock"),
        (36, 15, "airlock"), (19, 19, "airlock"), (8, 21, "airlock"),
        (19, 21, "airlock"), (31, 21, "airlock"), (20, 30, "airlock"),
        (13, 31, "airlock"), (16, 32, "airlock"), (20, 32, "airlock"),
        (24, 32, "airlock"),
        // ---- THE LIGHTS ------------------------------------------
        // Forty-nine fittings, one per bay of every room and corridor
        // on the station, each carrying the colour of the tube in it.
        // Standard ceiling tubes are tg's default fluorescent white
        // (LIGHT_COLOR_DEFAULT "#f3fffa", code/__DEFINES/colors.dm:254
        // -- the colour /obj/machinery/light ships with,
        // light.dm:24-28).
        //
        // The one warm fitting on the station is (20, 13): a tungsten
        // bulb (LIGHT_COLOR_TUNGSTEN "#FFD6AA", colors.dm:312) somebody
        // put in over the middle of the Commons and nobody has
        // replaced. Four tiles from the tube at (16, 13), so the deck
        // between them is a gradient from cool to warm and back --
        // the whole point of coloured light, standing on one floor.
        (6, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (11, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (16, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (22, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (28, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (33, 4, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (6, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (11, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (16, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (22, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (28, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (33, 9, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (6, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (10, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (14, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (21, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (25, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (29, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (33, 11, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (7, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (12, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (16, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (20, 13, "light", { "color": "#FFD6AA", "mount": "north" }), (23, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (27, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (32, 13, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (7, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (12, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (16, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (23, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (27, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (32, 18, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (5, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (10, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (15, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (18, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (24, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (28, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (32, 20, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (20, 22, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (24, 22, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (28, 23, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "west" }),
        (35, 23, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "east" }), (5, 25, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }),
        (11, 25, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "south" }), (5, 27, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }),
        (11, 27, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "north" }), (28, 27, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "west" }),
        (35, 28, "light", { "color": "#F3FFFA", "mount": "east" }),
        // ---- THE NINE APCs ---------------------------------------
        // One per room the station lights, each on the wall it is
        // mounted with its placement's `mount` property and each labelled
        // with the room it serves (`labels` below).
        //
        // EVERY ONE KEEPS L1 ON ITS RESERVE. A panel running off its
        // own cell feeds only the output layers flagged here and lets
        // the rest go dark (docs/power/map-format.md), and on this
        // station L1 is the room circuit -- every port, every fitting
        // and every door feed an APC owns is on it, where L2 is the
        // backbone the panel takes FROM. Flag L1 and a blackout is a
        // dim room; flag nothing and it is a dark one.
        //
        // NO CARD IS WRITTEN either, and none needs to be: a panel
        // carries a network socket now, and a socket seats its
        // lowest-tier part by default
        // (docs/map-properties/placements.md). That is what gives every
        // APC an address, and every room's plant and fittings a hub to
        // hang off without an engineer joining a single one of them.
        (4, 5, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (14, 5, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (26, 5, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (18, 11, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "north" }),
        (15, 14, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (4, 22, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (18, 22, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "north" }),
        (28, 22, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        (4, 27, "apc", { "l1_reserve": true, "mount": "west" }),
        // ---- ACCESS POINTS ---------------------------------------
        // Engineering's own, on the wall of the tank rank one cell of
        // cord from the port at (9, 27). Its lock is keyed to the
        // department, and that is a fact about this PLACEMENT rather
        // than about the entry -- the same row an airlock carries, so
        // one entry stands up a rack of nodes keyed to different claims
        // (docs/DEVICES.md SS8).
        (8, 27, "access_point", { "access": "engineering", "mount": "north" }),
        (22, 18, "access_point", { "mount": "south" }),
        (24, 6, "access_point", { "mount": "east" }),
        // ---- THE COMMONS DISPLAYS ---------------------------------
        // The shift clock and the board beside it share the north wall,
        // between the room's two light fittings, where everybody waking
        // in the Commons can see the time and whatever the board says.
        (21, 13, "digital_clock", { "mount": "north" }),
        (22, 13, "status_display", { "mount": "north" }),
        // ---- THE UTILITY OUTLET PORTS ----------------------------
        // A port is what everything on a cord plugs into, and on this
        // station that is the light fittings as much as the appliances
        // (docs/POWER.md, "Outlets and cords") -- so every bay with a
        // tube in it has one within cord reach, and a room with no port
        // is a room with no light.
        //
        // They stand on the deck rather than hanging on a wall, so a
        // placement here is the tile the bollard occupies and nothing
        // below owes it a facing.
        //
        // The three the crew think of as sockets, where somebody would
        // actually put an appliance down:
        (21, 4, "utility_outlet_port"), (24, 14, "utility_outlet_port"), (24, 29, "utility_outlet_port"),
        // ...and one per bay for the fittings overhead.
        (12, 4, "utility_outlet_port"), (32, 4, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (10, 9, "utility_outlet_port"), (21, 9, "utility_outlet_port"), (32, 9, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (20, 11, "utility_outlet_port"), (31, 11, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (10, 13, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (9, 18, "utility_outlet_port"), (17, 18, "utility_outlet_port"), (30, 18, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (21, 20, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (26, 22, "utility_outlet_port"), (28, 26, "utility_outlet_port"),
        (9, 27, "utility_outlet_port"), (35, 27, "utility_outlet_port"),
        // ...and one for the rank of cadaver chambers at (14, 6..8),
        // which are corded appliances and not just furniture. It sits
        // on the lab's own L1 loop at the south-west corner, two to
        // four cells of cord from each chamber -- the pair already in
        // this room, at (21, 4) and (21, 9), are nine and ten cells
        // away, which is past `cord_range` (docs/POWER.md, "Outlets
        // and cords"). A dark rank of chambers is what a mapper gets
        // for forgetting this, which is the point of the rule.
        (15, 9, "utility_outlet_port"),
        // ---- THE POWER PLANT -------------------------------------
        // Ten photovoltaic panels north of the hull where the sun is:
        // quiet, modest watts, no moving parts, and happy in vacuum.
        (6, 1, "solar"), (8, 1, "solar"), (10, 1, "solar"), (12, 1, "solar"),
        (14, 1, "solar"), (16, 1, "solar"), (18, 1, "solar"), (20, 1, "solar"),
        (22, 1, "solar"), (24, 1, "solar"),
        // ...and Engineering's own pair, standing on the circuit they
        // charge. The bank is BIDIRECTIONAL on layer 2 -- it draws
        // from the backbone when there is surplus and feeds it back
        // when there is not, which is what makes it a buffer rather
        // than a load (docs/POWER.md).
        (6, 22, "smes", { "l2": "bidirectional" }),
        (10, 22, "generator"),
        // The gravitational generator, standing in the middle of
        // Engineering under the SMES and the generator it sits between.
        // The tile named is the BOTTOM-MIDDLE one, tg's part 8 -- the
        // machine claims the 3x3 block above it, (7..9, 23..25)
        // (gravitygenerator.dm:179-198). Nothing else stands in that
        // block; the L1 room circuit runs under it, which is where
        // cable belongs. It arrives RUNNING, as tg's plain `main` does
        // (`:118`); `main/off` is the variant a mapper picks for a
        // nonfunctional machine. World gravity comes from the selected
        // profile, and this station's saved preference keeps it standard.
        (8, 25, "gravity_generator", "on"),
        // ---- THE SUPPLY BOTTLES ----------------------------------
        // Eight canisters, each standing on a connector port and each
        // arriving CHARGED and OPEN: `air` names the recipe they hold
        // (`canister_air`, tg's own 21/79 at 45 atmospheres) and `open`
        // is the valve. A bottle that said neither would open the shift
        // empty with the valve shut, which is what an empty bottle
        // looks like -- so the fact that this station's distribution
        // main is pressurised at roundstart is a sentence somebody
        // wrote here rather than a thing the engine did.
        (8, 17, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }), (9, 17, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }),
        (10, 17, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }), (11, 17, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }),
        (15, 22, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }), (17, 22, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }),
        (19, 25, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }), (23, 26, "canister", { "blend": "canister_air", "open": true }),
        // The odd one out, on the free tile between two air bottles: the
        // same shell with ten litres of WATER in it and the valve shut.
        // A canister is a shell and a valve; the charge decides what it
        // is a bottle of. Shut because a valve that let a liquid out
        // would be a mechanism nobody has built yet -- opening it vents
        // the headspace and leaves the slug standing.
        (16, 22, "canister", { "blend": "water_10l" }),
        // ---- the two floor drains --------------------------------
        // Half a litre a second off the deck each
        // (content/structures/floor_drain.luau), and the station has two
        // because it has exactly two places where litres end up on the
        // floor rather than in a shell.
        //
        // One under the water reservoir at (12, 28). That tank is the
        // one store here with a liquid phase, and the comment above it
        // says what happens when somebody plumbs it into a warm room: it
        // rains, on this deck. The grating is where the rain lands.
        //
        // One in front of the chem bench, a tile south of the burner at
        // (20, 5). A chemist tips glassware; the beakers on that bench
        // are already full at roundstart. Neither drain is plumbed to
        // anything, because a drain is not: what goes down it leaves the
        // world through the engine's `drained` register rather than
        // arriving somewhere else.
        (12, 29, "floor_drain"),
        (20, 6, "floor_drain"),
    ],
    // The two vestibule interlocks: each airlock names the direction its
    // partner lies in, and the link resolves only because BOTH of them
    // do (tg airlock.dm:225 `cyclelinkairlock()`, whose scan rejects a
    // door that does not point back). Opening either shuts the other.
    cycle_links: [
        (3, 15, East),
        (5, 15, West),
        (34, 15, East),
        (36, 15, West),
    ],
    // The station's vending machines stand finished at roundstart on
    // their departments' powered walls, with a socket in cord range --
    // unplug the room and shopping stops, which is the point of corded
    // appliances.
    machines: [
        (23, 14, "fax"),
        (24, 18, "photocopier"),
        (21, 6, "fax"),
        (24, 13, "vendor"),
        (24, 15, "tool_vendor"),
        // The third crate against the same wall, on the same port.
        // tg puts Good Clean Fun in the dormitory or the library; this
        // station has neither, and the Commons is the room a shift
        // actually crosses.
        (24, 16, "games_vendor"),
        // The chem bench, on the Chemistry Lab's north wall beside the room's
        // own port at (21, 4) -- one and two tiles of cord, well inside
        // the six a corded appliance gets (docs/POWER.md). The port
        // stands on the north rank's own deck, on the L1 loop that runs
        // under it, which is where the bench's cords can reach. A dispenser,
        // reaction chamber and packager standing together are the whole of
        // what a chemist needs: press, pour, heat, and make portable goods.
        //
        // In this lab rather than the Commons because the first draft
        // put it on the card tables, and a rec room whose tables are
        // occupied by glassware is not a rec room. This was the one
        // wired, lit, ventilated room with nothing in it that is
        // not reserved for growth (the Cargo Bay and Cargo Hall stay
        // empty on purpose) -- so the empty room becomes the lab, one
        // door off the Concourse.
        (19, 4, "chem_dispenser"),
        (20, 4, "chem_heater"),
        (21, 4, "chem_packager"),
        // Medical equipment and chemistry clothing stand beside the
        // bench, matching tg's medbay-storage and chemistry placement.
        // Both are within three tiles of the lab port at (21, 4).
        (23, 4, "nanomed_plus"),
        (24, 4, "chemdrobe"),
        // The operating computer, one tile south of the medical shelf
        // and three of the lab's own port at (21, 4). tg stands one
        // beside an operating table and so does this: the table is at
        // (24, 5), one of the four tiles this console reads. It stands
        // in the open rather than on the wall rank so the other three
        // stay usable -- a patient laid on the deck at (22, 5) or
        // (23, 6) is a patient it can see too.
        (23, 5, "operating_computer"),
        // The pipe dispenser, corded to the port beside it. Every
        // fitting the department is built out of comes off this shelf,
        // which is why it stands in the department rather than in the
        // Commons with the snacks.
        (25, 29, "pipe_dispenser"),
        // The network router, on Engineering's own port at (9, 27) --
        // two cells of cord. A root tops a tree and hears the whole of
        // it (docs/DEVICES.md SS5), so the one rack on the station that
        // is listening to every segment stands in the department that
        // owns the network rather than in a corridor.
        (7, 27, "network_router", { "access": "engineering" }),
        // The station's two radio transceivers, each with its
        // department's key already seated (the variant's own
        // `encryption_1`) and its other sockets on the plain rungs.
        // Engineering's stands beside the router on the department's
        // own port at (9, 27) -- the tower and the rack that would
        // network it in the same closet, though joining them is panel
        // work somebody still has to do. Medical's stands on the
        // Chemistry Lab's north rank, four tiles of cord from the
        // lab's port at (21, 4), where the crew who most need to be
        // reachable at a distance actually work. Security and command
        // ship as variants only; a shift that wants those words on
        // the air builds for them.
        (6, 27, "transceiver.engineering"),
        (17, 4, "transceiver.medical"),
        // The Common tower stands in the Commons itself, on the north
        // rank beside the vendor corner -- two tiles of cord from the
        // port at (24, 14) -- so the channel the whole crew's
        // headsets ship on keeps working at range from the first tick
        // of the shift. Without it, Common is a ten-tile bubble.
        (22, 13, "transceiver.common"),
        // The programming terminal, at the blind east end of the same
        // rack room -- three cells of cord from the port at (9, 27),
        // and past the two doorways at (6, 26) and (10, 26) rather than
        // standing under either of them, because a dense machine in a
        // doorway is a wall somebody has to walk around forever.
        //
        // It is in Engineering because Engineering is where the network
        // is, and a disk somebody wrote at a terminal in a corridor
        // would have nowhere to be carried TO.
        (12, 27, "programming_terminal"),
    ],
